  "assessment_summary": "This paper evaluates a well-designed RCT in Punjab, India, testing whether payments for ecosystem services (PES) contracts with partial upfront payments can reduce crop residue burning more effectively than standard (fully conditional) PES contracts. The study is motivated by the enormous health costs of agricultural burning in North India and the failure of existing regulatory approaches. The core finding is striking: despite receiving lower conditional payments, farmers offered upfront PES are 8-12 percentage points more likely to comply than those offered standard PES, and remote sensing data confirms that upfront PES significantly reduced burning relative to the control group, while standard PES had no measurable effect on actual burning (i.e., payments were inframarginal). The paper makes several important contributions.\n\nStrengths: (1) The research question is highly policy-relevant, addressing a major source of air pollution affecting hundreds of millions of people. (2) The experimental design is clean, with village-level randomization, a pre-analysis plan, and multiple treatment arms that allow comparisons of contract designs. (3) The use of novel high-resolution satellite imagery (PlanetScope and Sentinel-2) combined with machine learning to measure burning outcomes for all farmers (including control) is innovative and addresses a key limitation of relying solely on administrative compliance data. (4) The paper presents multiple outcome measures (contract compliance, two remote sensing classifications, endline survey CRM practices) that tell a consistent story. (5) The cost-effectiveness analysis, comparing PES costs to mortality benefits and alternative abatement options, is highly informative for policy.\n\nLimitations and concerns: (1) The remote sensing classification model has only 78-82% accuracy, and the two threshold choices yield meaningfully different control group burning rates (9% vs. 20%) and somewhat different treatment effect magnitudes. While the authors show treatment effects are less sensitive to threshold choice than control means, the cost-effectiveness calculations are sensitive to these choices, particularly for the standard PES arm where effects are near zero. (2) The RF model is trained using negative labels only from treatment group monitoring data, which could introduce bias if the spectral signature of not-burning differs by treatment status. The authors provide distributional checks (Figure A.3) but cannot fully rule out this concern. (3) The mechanism analysis is relatively weak. The pre-specified heterogeneity analysis using baseline indices of trust and liquidity constraints is uninformative (null results). The endline trust measure showing a 7 percentage point difference between upfront and standard PES is suggestive but endogenous—farmers who received upfront money and then received conditional payments may simply report higher trust post-hoc. The paper cannot cleanly separate the trust channel from other possible mechanisms (reciprocity, sunk cost effects, attention/salience). (4) There is differential attrition in the endline survey (~5 pp more in treatment than control), though not between standard and upfront arms. Lee bounds are appropriately provided but widen confidence intervals. (5) The sample is deliberately selected for high burning propensity (cooperative members, 2-12 acres, using equipment indicative of burning), which aids statistical power but limits external validity. The authors acknowledge this but could discuss more thoroughly how effects might differ in a broader population. (6) The monitoring protocol requires farmers to call to initiate monitoring, which creates an additional compliance barrier beyond not-burning itself. This likely depresses measured compliance rates and makes the compliance measure a composite of not-burning and successfully navigating the monitoring process. (7) The cost-effectiveness comparison with coal scrubbers, while illustrative, involves very different assumptions and is somewhat apples-to-oranges—the PES program was a one-time small-scale RCT, and scaling would likely change both costs and effects as noted in the conclusion. (8) The back-of-envelope mortality calculations use a $1 million VSL which, while reasonable for India, is not discussed in terms of its uncertainty or sensitivity. (9) The paper is a single-season study with no information on persistence of effects or how repeated implementation might change behavior or trust. (10) The total sample size is moderate (1,668 farmers across 171 villages), and some treatment arms have relatively small samples (e.g., 272 for the ₹1600 arm), limiting the ability to detect differences between sub-treatments.\n\nOverall, this is a strong applied paper addressing a first-order policy problem with a rigorous experimental design and innovative measurement approach. The main result—that upfront payments dramatically improve PES compliance despite lower conditional incentives—is robust across multiple outcome measures and has clear policy implications. The inability to definitively pin down mechanisms is a limitation but does not undermine the policy relevance of the finding. The paper is well-written, transparent about limitations, and provides useful cost-effectiveness comparisons.",
